<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Northern England</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as North of England</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, or the North of England, is the northern part of England, from the Scottish border to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a>, including cities such as Manchester, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a>, Leeds, Sheffield and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne">Newcastle</a>. The heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, wool, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> and shipbuilding, it suffered heavily from the decline of these industries in the twentieth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/northern-england.jpg" alt="Northern England" width="320" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Smurrayinchester · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Northern_England.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> mainly corresponds to the historic counties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheshire">Cheshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumberland">Cumberland</a>, Durham, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a>, Westmorland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a>. For statistical purposes it is the three northernmost regions of England: the North East, the North West, and Yorkshire and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humber">Humber</a>. Its northern boundary is the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, its western the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-sea">Irish Sea</a> and a short border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, and its eastern the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>. Its southern border is often debated.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennines">Pennines</a>, sometimes called the backbone of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, run through most of the area, from the Tyne Gap to the Peak District. Other uplands are the Lake District, which has England's highest mountains, the Cheviot Hills and the North York Moors. The Pennines hold peat, large coalfields and millstone grit. The major cities are all as recent as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a>, while older cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Chester</a>, Durham, Lancaster and York are comparatively small.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Roman histories name the Brigantes, whose name probably means highlanders, as the Celtic tribe that held most of the region, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigantia">Brigantia</a>. After Roman rule ended and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a> arrived, the Old North, Yr Hen Ogledd, divided into the rival kingdoms of Bernicia, Deira, Rheged and Elmet. Aethelfrith of Bernicia conquered Deira around 604 and united the two as Northumbria, which saw a golden age of scholarly and monastic activity centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a> with the help of Irish monks. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbric">Cumbric</a>, a Celtic language, was spoken mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbria">Cumbria</a> until about the 12th century.</p><p>The Northumbrian and Danish aristocracy resisted the Norman Conquest, and in the winter of 1069 to 1070 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> ordered the Harrying of the North, in which towns, villages and farms were systematically destroyed across much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a> and County Durham. Famine followed. Chroniclers reported a hundred thousand deaths, while modern estimates are in the tens of thousands out of a population of two million. After the English Reformation the North saw Catholic risings against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a>, including the Pilgrimage of Grace, and the Rising of the North against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a>.</p><p>At the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a> the North had plentiful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and water power and cheap labour. Textile manufacture, iron and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a>, and shipyards on Tyneside and at Barrow-in-Furness grew up. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> was the turning point for the economy. In 1937 unemployment in the North, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> was 16.1 per cent against 7.1 per cent in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and the period saw unrest including the general strike of 1926 and the Jarrow March.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The three regions together covered about 37,331 square kilometres and had a population of 15.5 million at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, with 18 cities. Traditional dialect areas follow the historic counties, among them Cumbrian, Lancastrian, Northumbrian and Tyke, and urban speech includes Geordie, Mancunian and Scouse.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Northern England” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Between the wars the old staple industries of coal, cotton, shipbuilding and iron declined, leaving mass unemployment in South Wales, the <b>North of England</b>, Clydeside and Northern Ireland; the General Strike of May 1926 and the Jarrow March of 1936 became symbols of the era.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom">United Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>There is evidence that the carol comes from the <b>North of England</b>, in particular the area around Newcastle upon Tyne: William Henry Husk wrote in 1864 that it was &quot;found on broadsides printed at Newcastle at various periods during the last hundred and fifty years&quot;, and many 19th-century…</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-twelve-days-of-christmas">The Twelve Days of Christmas</a></li></ul></section>
